#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:1
msgid "Feature Name: `homogeneous_try_blocks`"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:2
msgid "Start Date: 2024-02-22"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:3
msgid ""
"RFC PR: [rust-lang/rfcs#3721](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/3721)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:4
msgid ""
"Rust Issue: [rust-lang/rust#154391](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/"
"issues/154391)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:6
msgid "Summary"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:9
msgid ""
"Tweak the behaviour of `?` inside `try{}` blocks to not depend on context, "
"in order to work better with methods and need type annotations less often."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:12
msgid "The stable behaviour of `?` when _not_ in a `try{}` block is untouched."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:15
msgid "Motivation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:18
msgid ""
"I do have some mild other concerns about try block -- in particular it is "
"frequently necessary in practice to give hints as to the try of a try-block."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:21
msgid ""
"~ [Niko commenting on #70941](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/"
"issues/70941#issuecomment-612167041)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:25
msgid "The desugaring of `val?` currently works as follows, per RFC #3058:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:34
msgid ""
"Importantly, that's using a trait to create the return value. And because "
"the argument of the associated function is a generic on the trait, it "
"depends on inference to determine the correct type to return."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:38
msgid ""
"That works great in functions, because Rust's inference trade-offs mean that "
"the return type of a function is always specified in full.  Thus the "
"`return` has complete type context, both to pick the return type as well as, "
"for `Result`, the exact error type into which to convert the error."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:43
msgid ""
"However, once things get more complicated, it stops working as well.  That's "
"even true before we start adding `try{}` blocks, since closures can hit them "
"too. (While closures behave like functions in most ways, their return types "
"can be left for type inference to figure out, and thus might not have full "
"context.)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:48
msgid ""
"For example, consider this example of trying to use `Iterator::try_for_each` "
"to read the `Result`s from the `BufRead::lines` iterator:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:56 src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:59
msgid "// <-- question mark\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:66
msgid "Though it looks reasonable, it doesn't compile:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:68
msgid ""
"```text\n"
"error[E0282]: type annotations needed\n"
" --> src/lib.rs:7:9\n"
"  |\n"
"7 |         Ok(())\n"
"  |         ^^ cannot infer type for type parameter `E` declared on the enum "
"`Result`\n"
"  |\n"
"\n"
"error[E0283]: type annotations needed\n"
" --> src/lib.rs:8:7\n"
"  |\n"
"8 |     })?; // <-- question mark\n"
"  |       ^ cannot infer type for type parameter `E`\n"
"  |\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:84
msgid ""
"The core of the problem is that there's nothing to constrain the "
"intermediate type that occurs _between_ the two `?`s.  We'd be happy for it "
"to just be the same `io::Result<_>` as in the other places, but there's "
"nothing saying it _must_ be that. To the compiler, we might want some "
"completely different error type that happens to support conversion to and "
"from `io::Error`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:90
msgid ""
"The easiest fix here is to annotate the return type of the closure, as "
"follows:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:96
msgid "// <-- return type\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:107
msgid ""
"But it would be nice to have a way to request that \"the obvious thing\" "
"should happen."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:109
msgid ""
"This same kind of problem happens with `try{}` blocks as they were "
"implemented in nightly at the time of writing of this RFC.  The desugaring "
"of `?` in a `try{}` block was essentially the same as in a function or "
"closure, differing only in that it \"returns\" the value from the block "
"instead of from the enclosing function."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:114
msgid ""
"For example, this works great if the type context is available from the "
"return type:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:124
msgid ""
"Suppose, however, that you wanted to do more in the method after the "
"additions, and thus added a `try{}` block around it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:130
msgid "// pre-RFC version\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:139
msgid ""
"That doesn't compile, since a (non-trait) method call required the type be "
"determined:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:141
msgid ""
"```text\n"
"error[E0282]: type annotations needed\n"
" --> src/lib.rs:3:5\n"
"  |\n"
"3 | /     try { // pre-RFC version\n"
"4 | |         x?.checked_add(y?)?.checked_add(z?)?\n"
"5 | |     }\n"
"  | |_____^ cannot infer type\n"
"  |\n"
"  = note: type must be known at this point\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:153
msgid ""
"This is, in a way, more annoying than the `Result` case.  Since at least "
"there, there's the possibility that one wants the `io::Error` converted into "
"some `my_special::Error`.  But for `Option`, there's no conversion for "
"`None`. While it's possible that there's some other type that accepts its "
"residual, the normal case is definitely that it just stays a `None`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:159
msgid ""
"This RFC proposes using the unannotated `try { ... }` block as the marker to "
"request a slightly-different `?` desugaring that stays in the same family."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:162
msgid ""
"With that, the `adding_b` example just works.  And the earlier "
"`concat_lines` problem can be solved simply as"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:169
msgid "// <-- new version of `try`\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:177
msgid ""
"(Note that this version also removes an `Ok(())`, as was decided in "
"[\\#70941](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/70941).)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:181
msgid "Guide-level explanation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:184
msgid ""
"_Assuming this would go some time after [9.2](https://doc.rust-lang.org/"
"stable/book/ch09-02-recoverable-errors-with-result.html) in the book, which "
"introduces `Result` and `?` for error handling._"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:189
msgid ""
"So far all the places we've used `?` it's been fine to just return from the "
"function on an error.  Sometimes, however, it's nice to do a bunch of "
"fallible operations, but still handle the errors from all of them before "
"leaving the function."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:192
msgid ""
"One way to do that is to make a closure and immediately call it (an _IIFE_, "
"immediately-invoked function expression, to borrow a name from JavaScript):"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:197
#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:226
#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:261
msgid "\"hello\""
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:198
#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:227
#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:262
msgid "\"world\""
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:203
msgid ""
"That's somewhat symbol soup, however.  And even worse, it doesn't actually "
"compile because it doesn't know what error type to use:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:216
msgid ""
"Why haven't we had this problem before?  Well, when we're writing "
"_functions_ we have to write the return type of the function down "
"explicitly.  The `?` operator in a function uses that to know to which error "
"type it should convert any error it gets. But in the closure, the return "
"type is left to be inferred, and there are many possible answers, so "
"compilation fails because of the ambiguity."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:222
msgid "This can be fixed by using a _try block_ instead:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:232
msgid ""
"Here the `?` operator still does essentially the same thing -- either gives "
"the value from the `Ok` or short-circuits the error from the `Err` -- but "
"with slightly different details:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:236
msgid ""
"Rather than returning the error from the function, it returns it from the "
"`try` block. And thus in this case an error from either `read_to_string` "
"ends up in the `pair_result` local."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:239
msgid ""
"Rather than using the function's return type to decide the error type, it "
"keeps using the same family as the type to which the `?` was applied. And "
"thus in this case, since `read_to_string` returns `io::Result<String>`, it "
"knows to return `io::Result<_>`, which ends up being `io::Result<(String, "
"String)>`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:244
msgid ""
"The trailing expression of the `try` block is automatically wrapped in "
"`Ok(...)`, so we get to remove that call too.  (Note to RFC readers: this "
"decision is not part of this RFC. It was previously decided in [\\#70941]"
"(https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/70941).)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:248
msgid ""
"This behaviour is what you want in the vast majority of simple cases.  In "
"particular, it always works for things with just one `?`, so simple things "
"like `try { a? + 1 }` will do the right thing with minimal syntactic "
"overhead.  It's also common to want to group a bunch of things with the same "
"error type.  Perhaps it's a bunch of calls to one library, which all use "
"that library's error type.  Or you want to do [a bunch of `io` operations]"
"(https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/"
"d6f3a4ecb48ead838638e902f2fa4e5f3059779b/compiler/rustc_borrowck/src/nll."
"rs#L355-L367) which all use `io::Result`.  Additionally, `try` blocks work "
"with `?`\\-on-`Option` as well, where error-conversion is never needed, "
"since there is only `None`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:256
msgid ""
"It will fail to compile, however, if not everything shares the same error "
"type. Suppose we add some formatting operation to the previous example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:268
msgid "The compiler won't let us do that:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:270
msgid ""
"```text\n"
"error[E0308]: mismatched types\n"
"  --> src/lib.rs:14:32\n"
"   |\n"
"   |     let c: i32 = b.parse()?;\n"
"   |                           ^ expected struct `std::io::Error`, found "
"struct `ParseIntError`\n"
"   = note: expected enum `Result<_, std::io::Error>`\n"
"              found enum `Result<_, ParseIntError>`\n"
"note: return type inferred to be `Result<_, std::io::Error>` here\n"
"  --> src/lib.rs:14:32\n"
"   |\n"
"   |     let a = std::fs::read_to_string(\"hello\")?;\n"
"   |                                             ^\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:285
msgid ""
"For now, the best solution for that mixed-error case is the same as before: "
"to refactor it to a function."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:287
msgid "Common `Option` Patterns"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:289
msgid ""
"Various languages with `null` have a _null-conditional_ operator `?.` that "
"short-circuits if the value to the left is `null`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:291
msgid ""
"Rust, of course, doesn't have `null`, but `None` often serves a similar "
"role. `try` blocks plus `?` combine to give Rust a `?.` without needing to "
"add it as a special operator."
msgstr ""

