msgid "but with `try` blocks, you simplify that down to"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:332
msgid ""
"You can also use this for things that don't have dedicated methods on "
"`Option`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:334
msgid ""
"For example, there's an `Option::zip` for going from `Option<A>` and "
"`Option<B>` to `Option<(A, B)>`. But there's no _three_\\-argument version "
"of this."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:337
msgid "That's ok, though, since you can do that with `try` blocks easily:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:344
msgid "Reference-level explanation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:347
msgid ""
"⚠️ This section describes a possible implementation that works with today's "
"type system. ⚠️"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:349
msgid ""
"The core of the RFC is the homogeneity of `try` blocks.  As the author of "
"the RFC, I'd be happy with other implementations that maintain the "
"properties of this one. If it ended up happening with custom typing rules "
"instead, or something, that would be fine. But it's worth emphasizing that "
"it's doable entirely via a desugaring, no new solver features."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:354
msgid "Grammar"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:356
msgid "No change to the grammar; it stays just"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:358
msgid "_TryBlockExpression_: `try` _BlockExpression_"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:360
msgid "Desugaring"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:362
msgid ""
"Today on nightly, `x?` _inside a `try` block_ desugars as follows, after "
"[RFC 3058](https://rust-lang.github.io/rfcs/3058-try-trait-v2.html):"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:373
msgid ""
"Where `'try` means the synthetic label added to the innermost enclosing "
"`try` block. (The actual label is not something that can be mentioned from "
"user code, but it's using the same [label-break-value](https://blog.rust-"
"lang.org/2022/11/03/Rust-1.65.0.html#break-from-labeled-blocks) mechanism "
"that stabilized in 1.65.)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:379
msgid "This RFC changes that desugaring to"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:382
msgid ""
"// This is an internal convenience function for the desugar, not something "
"public\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:393
msgid ""
"This still uses `FromResidual::from_residual` to actually create the value, "
"but determines the type to return from the argument via the `Residual` trait "
"rather than depending on having sufficient context to infer it."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:397
msgid "The `Residual` trait"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:399
msgid ""
"This trait [already exists as unstable](https://doc.rust-lang.org/1.94.0/std/"
"ops/trait.Residual.html), so feel free to read its rustdoc instead of here, "
"if you prefer.  It was added to support APIs like [`Iterator::try_find`]"
"(https://doc.rust-lang.org/1.82.0/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html#method."
"try_find) which also need this \"I want a `Try` type from the same 'family', "
"but with a different `Output` type\" behaviour."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:404
msgid ""
"⚠️ As the author of this RFC, the details of this trait are not the important "
"part of this RFC. ⚠️ I propose that, like was done for [RFC 3058](https://"
"rust-lang.github.io/rfcs/3058-try-trait-v2.html), the exact details here be "
"left as an unresolved question to be finalized after nightly "
"experimentation. In particular, it appears that the [naming and structure "
"related to `try_trait_v2` is likely to change](https://github.com/rust-lang/"
"rust/issues/84277#issuecomment-1066120333), and thus the `Residual` trait "
"will likely change as part of that.  But for now this RFC is written "
"following the names used in the previous RFC."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:418
msgid "Implementations"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:435
msgid "Drawbacks"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:438
msgid ""
"This adds extra nuance to the `?` operator, so one might argue that the "
"extra convenience of homogeneity is not worth the complexity and that adding "
"type annotations instead is fine."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:442
msgid "Rationale and alternatives"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:445
msgid "Supporting methods"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:447
msgid ""
"Today on nightly, with potentially-heterogeneous `try` blocks, this code "
"doesn't work"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:455
msgid ""
"because method invocation requires that it knows the type, but with a "
"contextual return type from the `try` block that's not available"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:465
msgid ""
"With the homogeneous `try` blocks in this RFC, however, that works because "
"the type flows \"out\" from the try block, rather than \"in\" from how the "
"block is used."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:468
msgid "Supporting generics"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:470
msgid ""
"Essentially the same as the previous section, but this doesn't work on "
"nightly either:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:479
msgid ""
"because `dbg!` accepts any `Debug`gable type and thus here it also doesn't "
