msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:496
msgid "Homogeneous `try` fixes this as well."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:498
msgid "The simple case deserves the simple syntax"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:500
msgid ""
"We could add a new `try homogeneous { ... }` block with this behaviour, and "
"leave `try { ... }` as heterogeneous."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:502
msgid ""
"That feels backwards, because heterogeneous try blocks are the ones that "
"most commonly need a _type_ annotation of some sort."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:504
msgid ""
"If there's `?`s on multiple `Result`s with incompatible error types, we need "
"to tell it _somehow_ which type to use. Maybe we want an `anyhow::"
"Result<_>`, maybe we want our own `Result<_, crate::CustomError>`, whatever."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:507
msgid ""
"Thus if they commonly need a type annotation anyway, we can consider in the "
"future (see below for more) an annotated version of `try` blocks that allow "
"heterogeneity, while leaving the short thing for the simple case."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:510
msgid "Manual error conversion is always possible"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:512
msgid ""
"Even inside a homogeneous `try` block, you could always _manually_ add a "
"call to convert an error."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:514
msgid "For example, you could do something like"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:523
msgid ""
"if you need to convert the error type from `bar` to the one used by `foo` "
"and `qux`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:525
msgid ""
"We could always add a specific method to express that intent, though this "
"RFC does not propose one. Spelling it as `.map_err(into)` might be pretty "
"good already, which would be possible with [RFC#3591](https://github.com/"
"rust-lang/rfcs/pull/3591)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:530
msgid "Other merging approaches"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:532
msgid ""
"There's a variety of other things we could do if the `?`s don't all match."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:534
msgid "Maybe we try to convert everything to the first one"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:535
msgid "Maybe we try to convert everything to the last one"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:536
msgid ""
"Maybe we fold them through some type function that attempts to merge "
"residuals"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:538
msgid "But these are all much less local."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:540
msgid ""
"A nice property of the homogeneous `try` block is that you don't have to "
"think about all this stuff. When you see `try {`, you know that they're all "
"the same.  You can thus reorder them without worrying. So long as you know "
"what family one of them is from, you know the rest are the same."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:544
msgid "This case really is common"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:546
msgid ""
"The rust compiler uses `try` blocks in a bunch of places already.  Last I "
"checked, they were _all_ homogeneous. (Though of course it's possible that "
"some have been added since then.)"
msgstr ""

