#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:638
msgid "Why `Residual::TryType` isn't a GAT"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:640
msgid ""
"One might expect that, rather than having a generic parameter on the trait, "
"`Residual` would look like"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:648
msgid ""
"The reason it's not done that way is that today we have no way to let an "
"implementation add additional constraints for which types can be passed to "
"that GAT.  That means it'd be impossible to implement `Residual` for a type "
"that needed `Copy` or only supported `()`, for example."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:652
msgid ""
"Take this type, trying to match the common C idiom of \"zero is success; non-"
"zero is error\":"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:686
msgid ""
"The proposed trait structure lets us have that `impl Residual<()> for "
"CResultResidual`, whereas trying to implement some kind of"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:693
msgid ""
"just can't work because there's nowhere to put an arbitrary `V` in `CResult`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:695
msgid "Could we evolve this in future?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:697
msgid ""
"Because this is an early desugaring to existing features, this is the "
"easiest kind of thing to change over editions. There's no global system "
"changes involved, just a careful arrangement of trait calls."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:700
msgid ""
"That means that if it turns out that we ship it and find out over time that "
"it's not quite what we want, we could use an edition change to adjust it to "
"work differently.  It could use the edition of the `try` token to decide "
"which behaviour should apply, for example.  And any maintenance cost of "
"_this_ approach on previous editions would remain low, since it didn't need "
"any complex support in the first place."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:705
msgid ""
"For example, maybe in the future we could get new type system features that "
"would allow some kind of \"fallback hinting\" so that `try` blocks wouldn't "
"need the homogeneity restriction to compile in the common cases, and we "
"could thus over an edition change the desugaring to use that instead.  But "
"we don't need to wait for an unknown to ship something now; we can switch "
"how it works later easily enough."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:711
msgid "Prior art"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:714
msgid ""
"Languages with traditional exceptions don't return a value from `try` "
"blocks, so don't have this problem. Even checked exceptions are still always "
"the `Exception` type."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:717
msgid "Scoping of nullability checks"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:719
msgid ""
"In C#, the `?.` operator is scoped without a visible lexical block. We could "
"try to special-case `?.`, maybe over an edition change, to do something "
"similar instead of needing the `try { ... }` at all."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:722
msgid "The invisible scope can be trouble, however.  Take this program:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:741
msgid "// Check 1\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:742
msgid "\"FirstOnePassed\""
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:743
msgid "// Check 2\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:748
msgid ""
"The first check never actually runs, because the `?.` skips it, as it's "
"scoped to the statement. The second check fails, because the `?.` got scoped "
"to the parens."
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:751
msgid "Translating the two to Rust, they'd be"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:755
msgid "vs"
msgstr ""

#: src/3721-homogeneous-try-blocks.md:759
msgid ""
"where having the lexical scope visible emphasizes what happens if the `?` "
"does short-circuit."
msgstr ""

